213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (In Algorithms to Live By, Brian Christian and Tom Griffiths describe relaxation as a way of solving a hard problem by loosening one of its constraints, solving the easier version first, and working back towards the real one. I first thought about this while comparing boatyard sites, since no single spot on the coast gives full access and full shelter at the same time. Communities settle for a workable trade off rather than a perfect answer.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
214 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (I now apply the same idea to the wetland itself. It moves between wet and dry ground depending on the season, holding a trade off between water and land rather than staying fixed as one or the other. The Kundapura Master Plan cannot hold that kind of space. Its categories treat land as either developed, agricultural, marshy, or water, with nothing in between.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
