/E (THE FINAL IDEA)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 159 0 R
/E (The core idea is to compare satellite imagery of the Kundapur wetlands, gathered across seasons and across years, against the fixed categories laid out in the Master Plan. Talking to Jaya, I learned that tides at the estuary change roughly every four hours. The wetland is not only shifting slowly across decades, it is also moving within a single day. A single satellite image only catches one moment in that cycle, so no one image can stand in for the whole pattern. This is why I am pulling imagery across multiple timescales, short term tidal and seasonal shifts alongside longer term change going back at least 20 years, rather than relying on one before and after comparison.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
280 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 159 0 R
/E (This means bringing the imagery into QGIS to trace where the wetland's boundary actually falls at each point in time. Where ready made boundary files are not available, I will trace these by hand using the satellite imagery itself. Once that boundary map exists, I lay the Master Plan's four categories, Developed Area, Agricultural Area, Marshy Area, and Water Sheet, over it, to see where the two diverge. The comparison is grounded in how communities actually use this land, drawing on accounts like Jaya's about shellfish farming and shifting fishing patterns, so the mismatch is read against daily life and not just against a drawing.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
